dore about wildlands I  seem to find one more I can't stand as  someone with a long history of playing  Tom Clancy titles and a longer history  of playing shooters this game has me  conflicted the way I see it wild lands  represents both the best and worst  aspects of Ubisoft's open-world games  and one year after Ghost Recon is  released both sides are all the more  pronounced  [Music]  [Music]  if you haven't played wildlands here's  the abbreviated plot for u.s. army  members drop into a bolivia overtaken by  the mexican drug cartel known as Santa  Blanca  if you speak basic Spanish then you know  how on the nose or even in the nose this  name is like can we get some legal coca  your mission is to take down El sueño  the head of Santa Blanca and any  underboss is standing in your way  all of this unfolds in a massive open  world much has been said about ubisoft's  and boxes from the historical  recreations of Assassin's Creed city to  the cascading mission structure of the  Far Cry franchise but wildlands version  of Bolivia is a fascinating marvel in  its own right civilians exist only as  window dressing targets to avoid as you  mow your way through the cartel villages  serve more as stages for firefights than  places I can actually imagine people  gathering there's also a general lack of  combat variety that increases the more  time you spend with a game but viewed as  a sandbox meant to encourage tactical  stealth wildlands is almost unparalleled  viewed from a distance the game most  closely resembles Metal Gear Solid 5 the  phantom pain the latter is the superior  game in most regards but there is a lot  to learn from Ghost Recon swirled when  clearing an enemy camp you not only have  to choose an angle of approach but also  how you'll escape in the aftermath you  can use your drone to scout a base  before sneaking in for close quarters  combat if there's a machine jamming your  drone you can perch on a nearby cliff  and Scout the base from above if you're  extracting a cartel lieutenant you can  plan your exfiltration route through the  surrounding marshes to a boat you left  idling upriver that will soon take you  to your hideout this all works so well  in these scenarios pop up so often  because of wildland strong sense of  geography the world slowly changes from  arid plateaus to mountainous farmland  snake-like canyons to vast flat salt  deserts there is also an impressive  sense of verticality as you descend  sloping coca field  to escape a cartel outpost high up on a  hill many of these thrills are amplified  in coop I can spot enemies for two of my  teammates directing them through the  alleyways in town squares below while  our forth number circles in a chopper  nearby ready to pick us up at a moment's  notice  other times we would split into teams of  two one to disable the power generator  the other to eliminate the cartel leader  before slipping away under cover of  darkness  the thing with wildlands is that despite  my adoration for many of its aspects  I recommend it with major caveats for  one wildlands is much better on higher  difficulties it makes the world feel  more dangerous encourages stealth and  evasion and turns the game from a power  fantasy into a survival scenario if  you're feeling particularly brave play  it on ghost mode which Institute's perma  death and resets all of your progress  each time you die lastly try to be  stealthy Ubisoft Paris designed wild  lands to allow for numerous approaches  and while that's great in theory the  game does not hold up in all-out combat  shooting can be clunky firefights become  monotonous and AI teammates  despite being vastly improved in the  months since the game's release aren't  always reliable companions I so strongly  prefer a stealthy approach in wild lands  that I wish you be soft had focused  solely on designing a stealth game it  feels as if half of the team wanted to  make a GTA clone allowing for hilarious  mishaps and vehicle based hijinks across  the map and to be clear these options  are present but they feel more like an  afterthought than an actual pillar of  design in many ways wildlands feels like  two separate parts rather than one  unified game and this dichotomy pervades  not only the gameplay but the narrative  as well on the one hand wildlands is a  cheesy action romp filled with cringy  dialogue and npc banter on the other  it's a lukewarm examination of the war  on drugs if you've seen clear and  present danger or the more recent  Sicario you'll probably find wildlands  to be lacking in the narrative  Department it attempts to tell a similar  story to these films but it stumbles by  pulling punches when it could hit hard  it's also missing a self-awareness that  makes a rock stars open worlds work so  well they find ways to rectify the  overall storyline with the frequent  chaos unfolding on screen now I don't  think every game about the war on drugs  needs to say the same thing or to align  with a certain worldview especially in  the case of wildlands it's a third  person shooter designed to be fun it is  designed to entertain what's frustrating  is that Ubisoft Paris so frequently  hints at compelling topics only to  backtrack immediately afterward listen  to this dialogue between Ghost Recon  squad members the Bolivian economy was a  wreck back then if people got into coke  just to survive here's one more instance  after I cleared out a pyro lucite mine  which the cartel used to refine the  ingredients to make cocaine of these  cases raised interesting questions can  you ever stop the drug trade are you  actually making things worse by being  here Ghost Recon protagonists are  dropped into a foreign country to follow  the orders of a revenge-seeking CIA  handler imagine if wild lands explored  that blind obedience these operatives  are doing their job yes but at what  point does that job cross the line in  one cutscene I recently saw and I  actually don't want to show here that  same CIA operative forces a cartel boss  to snort line after line of cocaine as a  form of torture she only allows him to  stop once he gives her the information  she's seeking when put up against  everything else you're doing in this  game and the hilarious situations you  often find yourself in it just felt a  little tone deaf in general I think  wildlands would be much better if it  allowed for player agency in the  narrative it often gives you freedom and  how you explore the map build your  character and clear enemy outposts so  why not extend that freedom into the  story as well ghost recounts boss fights  are probably the best example of this  inherent conflict after gaining enough  Intel in each of Bolivia's provinces you  have the chance to kill capture or frame  the local cartel boss the problem is you  don't get to choose you're forced into  specific scenarios that fly in the face  of the player choice you have  until that point for example I poured  almost all of my skill points into  stealth abilities improving my drones  recon powers and making my character  more silent overall this build served me  very well as I almost always opted for  stealth when I could but then I came  across a Foss flying a heavily armed  helicopter and could only complete the  mission by shooting him out of the sky  my stealth upgrades were all but useless  to be fair wildlands gave me access to  grenade launchers and light machineguns  in the planning phase that would have  made my job easier but at the same time  the game was telling me my choice to be  stealthy up until that point didn't mean  as much anymore  prior missions stress player choice but  this boss and many others took that all  away I just wish that agency made its  way into every corner of wildlands  framework what if you be soft designed a  full-fledged faction system throughout  the campaign one that lets you choose  between helping the rebels or the  state-run military what if you could  choose how to deal with each cartel  leader rather than following the orders  of an NPC across the map now I realize  this would spark numerous complications  for the developers and would be a much  more difficult project to make and not  every game needs to be Bioshock or spec  ops the line in the way it makes you  question your role as the player but  hear me out  wildlands is set in a part of the world  where the drug trade has played a large  part in shaping the culture and economy  for decades the subject is ripe for  exploration and it's not one that you  can half-ass but in wild lands Ubisoft  does exactly that there's a gaping chasm  between the complex stage wild land sets  and the things it actually makes you do  I don't envy the team of writers that  has to contextualize any open-world  story telling your own plot while  accounting for player freedom is a  massive challenge but recent titles such  as The Legend of Zelda breath of a wild  and horizons euro Don have shown that it  can be done they've shown that the  open-world structure can serve to  enhance the narrative not clash with it  in returning to Ghost Recon wildlands I  found a game that exemplifies my take on  Ubisoft's open-world portfolio a craft  on display is unparalleled in its sense  of place but there is still a huge hole  where the narrative should be games such  as Far Cry 5 and Assassin's Creed  origins have crept a little closer to  more meaningful open worlds  but the publisher still hasn't come  close to its full potential  if you be soft could just take that  extra step into complicated territory it  could make timeless pieces of  entertainment games that are fun and  nuanced games that give us more to talk  about as the months roll by I don't  think it's a fluke that wildlands came  and went last year without much buzz it  released on the tail end of a crowded  first quarter but it also had an  identity problem and it still does it  feels torn between an all-out romp  through a lukewarm world and a thrilling  stealth game with an amazing sense of  place I only wish you be soft had  carried through on its promise of player  choice I wish the team had stuck to a  more refined vision than trying to  appeal to every single player there is a  strong intricate skeleton of a game here  it just needs a heart  [Music]

